! The silicon of si-epsilon.in with an ultrasoft pseudopotential.
!
! Same cell, same k-grid, same wedge -- only the dataset changes, so the three
! files together isolate what ultrasoft and PAW add to a linear response: the
! augmentation charge's own response inside `drho` (`addusdbec`,
! `lr_addusddens`), the change `int3` that a perturbing potential makes to
! `D_ij` (`newdq`), the augmentation charge's *dipole* inside the position
! operator (`compute_qdipol`, `adddvepsi_us`), and -- for PAW -- the
! one-centre potential's response to `becsum` (`PAW_dpotential`).
!
! `ecutrho` is 8 x `ecutwfc` rather than 4: the augmentation charge is sharply
! peaked and is not representable on a dual-4 grid, and it is exactly that
! separation of the two grids the norm-conserving case never exercises.
!
! The reference is regenerated with the vendored `ph.x` rather than taken from
! a committed benchmark, because there is no committed `epsil` case on a
! non-magnetic PAW or ultrasoft silicon at all.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 2, celldm(1) = 10.20, nat = 2, ntyp = 1,
    ecutwfc = 20.0, ecutrho = 160.0
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-12
    mixing_beta = 0.7
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-n-rrkjus_psl.0.1.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Si 0.00 0.00 0.00
 Si 0.25 0.25 0.25
K_POINTS (automatic)
 4 4 4 1 1 1
